  "statement": "The debate over artificial intelligence is not about speculative extinction; it is about the quantifiable human cost of standing still. Every month that federal regulators and institutional incumbents delay open-source deployment and compute scaling, millions of patients suffer from untreated protein-folding pathologies and chronic diseases that automated drug discovery could solve. When the Food and Drug Administration takes two hundred and ten days on average to review standard medical device applications, human lives pay the administrative toll. Stagnation is not neutral safety; it is an active moral choice to let people die of solvable biological failures while bureaucrats debate sci-fi hypotheticals.\n\nCritics wrap their precautionary demands in the language of civilizational survival, yet their models rely on unfalsifiable eschatology rather than empirical compute scaling metrics. Real capability progression follows observable FLOP growth and reproducible benchmark evaluations, not boardrooms panicking over cinematic rogue agents. When AlphaFold mapped two hundred million protein structures in seventy-two hours, it did not trigger a systemic collapse; it delivered immediate, practical tools to molecular biologists across four hundred university labs worldwide. The enforcement mechanism for this stagnation is clear: centralized licensing boards backed by export controls that protect legacy software monopolies under the banner of risk management.\n\nDemanding a pause on frontier model training cedes the future of global science to centralized cartels and state-backed incumbents who profit from artificial scarcity. Open-source distribution democratizes access to superhuman intelligence faster than any closed corporate boardroom can monetize safety theater. The political coalition supporting these slowdowns unites protectionist lobbyists with risk-averse bureaucrats, while the coalition bearing the cost consists of patients waiting for cures and r