=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-desires-001] AI-Powered Abundance and Global Problem-Solving: Techno-solutionism — The node treats AI as a general-purpose solver for problems — scarcity, disease, inequality — that are fundamentally rooted in political, social, and institutional failures, not merely technical deficits.
- [acc-desires-001] AI-Powered Abundance and Global Problem-Solving: Optimism bias — The framing systematically discounts negative externalities, transition costs, and misuse scenarios in favor of a best-case trajectory, without probabilistic justification.
- [acc-intentions-100] Tie AI Governance Milestones to Empirical Compute Scaling Benchmarks: Proxy Fallacy (Misplaced Concreteness) — Treating compute as the definitive operationalization of AI capability conflates a measurable input metric with the complex emergent outputs governance actually needs to track; capability breakthroughs from architectural changes may not register in compute benchmarks.
- [acc-intentions-001] Achieving Capability Leadership Through Open Accountability: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as between decentralized open accountability versus centralized state-managed gatekeeping, ignoring hybrid or multi-stakeholder governance models that incorporate both speed and structured oversight.
- [acc-intentions-001] Achieving Capability Leadership Through Open Accountability: Equivocation — The node conflates 'open-source-aligned deployment' (transparency and accessibility) with 'accountability' (enforceable responsibility for harms), treating them as equivalent when they may be in direct tension—open proliferation can undermine accountability by diffusing responsibility.
- [acc-intentions-004] Mandating Show-Your-Work Transparency in AI-Driven Science: Analogical reasoning overreach — The 'showing your work in a math problem' analogy breaks down for complex AI systems where intermediate steps are high-dimensional vector operations, not human-interpretable logical steps, making the analogy misleading as a policy justification.
- [acc-beliefs-005] Reinforcement Learning Turns Static Models into Goal-Directed Agents: Reification / Conceptual Conflation — The node treats 'agentic behavior' and 'goal-directed' as well-defined, measurable outcomes of RL, when these terms remain deeply contested in the literature. Calling a model 'goal-directed' because it uses tools or reasons step-by-step conflates behavioral proxies with underlying cognitive architecture.
- [acc-intentions-005] Letting Free Markets Steer Technological Advancement: Naturalistic fallacy — Framing the market-technology cycle as 'natural' and 'self-reinforcing' implicitly treats its outcomes as inherently legitimate or good, bypassing the need for normative justification of who benefits and who bears costs.
- [acc-intentions-006] Deflecting Safety Concerns by Questioning Critics' Motives: Ad hominem (circumstantial) — The strategy attacks critics' presumed motives — fear, resentment, desire for stagnation — rather than addressing the empirical or logical content of their safety arguments.
- [acc-intentions-006] Deflecting Safety Concerns by Questioning Critics' Motives: Straw man — Reducing the diverse landscape of AI safety criticism to 'irrational fear' caricatures the strongest versions of those arguments, making them easier to dismiss without genuine rebuttal.

