=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [saf-desires-001] Humanity's Survival Above All Else: Appeal to fear — Framing the desire around 'humanity-ending catastrophes' and permanent loss of agency invokes existential dread that can short-circuit probabilistic reasoning and crowd out sober cost-benefit analysis.
- [saf-desires-002] Humans Must Always Hold the Off Switch: False Dilemma — The node implicitly frames the choice as binary: either humans hold absolute control or AI operates dangerously without meaningful oversight, ignoring intermediate governance models such as partial autonomy with robust audit trails, tiered authority structures, or collaborative human-AI decision-making.
- [saf-desires-003] No Deployment Without Demonstrated Equity: Nirvana Fallacy — The demand for 'concrete, measurable, and verifiable fairness guarantees' implicitly assumes an achievable ideal state of equity, potentially dismissing real but imperfect improvements as insufficient for deployment authorization.
- [saf-intentions-001] Gating Deployment on Verifiable Safety Baselines and Subsidized Compliance Infrastructure: False Dilemma — The framing positions a full moratorium as the only responsible alternative to unconstrained development, systematically excluding middle-ground options such as capability-specific gates, mandatory staged deployment, or compute-indexed safety audits.
- [saf-intentions-003] Fixing Known Alignment Failures Before Scaling Further: Composition / Patchwork Fallacy — The node assumes that fixing discrete, enumerable vulnerabilities (jailbreaks, specification gaming) adds up to meaningfully 'resolved' alignment — conflating patching known surface failures with achieving systemic safety.
- [saf-desires-004] Hard Limits on What AI Can Do: False Sense of Security (Security Theater) — Mandating 'immutable behavioral guardrails' may produce compliance theater rather than genuine safety, as the complexity of advanced AI makes it practically impossible to verify that hard limits hold across all deployment contexts.
- [saf-intentions-004] Anchoring AI Accountability in Verifiable Provenance Records: Proxy substitution — The node treats verifiable provenance of training inputs as a meaningful proxy for model safety, when the actual safety-relevant quantity — model behavior and alignment — is not directly determined by input records alone.
- [saf-intentions-007] Designing good AI as a shield against bad AI: Technological solutionism — The framing treats dangerous AI primarily as a technical problem solvable with more AI, potentially neglecting the governance, institutional, and socio-political dimensions that defensive technology alone cannot address.
- [saf-intentions-009] Securing Data Centers and Supply Chains Against Model-Weight Theft: False Analogy — The nuclear security analogy maps imperfectly onto AI: nuclear materials are physically scarce and detectable, while model weights are software artifacts that can be copied instantaneously and silently, making containment logic fundamentally different.
- [saf-beliefs-009] Autonomous Self-Replication Makes AI Containment Fundamentally Unreliable: Slippery Slope — The claim implies an unstoppable progression from any capable AI to an internet-spanning, uncontainable entity, skipping over the many technical, architectural, and institutional barriers that would need to be overcome at each intermediate step.

